n/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=10,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={10}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}={10}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=10.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=10.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-139535/selection/0c8b08d4b198d89c5fe756f861bd5b37e398d2a5b3237908210d9646370a666f" :answer/id #uuid "37db1ace-7c2a-521c-84d6-5535e035437f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139535/selection/05402db46107d70ece210f0bd5e24b332ae4f43bd474d592035c3e3c92625629" :answer/id #uuid "df5cbe66-44b4-57fe-8ac6-fe7370138f4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139535/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "250b8c4e-3b8f-55c0-8b27-8af2310d14e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139535/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer/id #uuid "23f4b3da-60e0-53cf-9387-2384249e21fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer