$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139547/selection/78d9ad3ee5adf79dd4a92b184547ad36db0708eb90cb46f51b56f5ad263f9dec" :answer/id #uuid "041c8409-90ca-5960-bb66-d08f274d248d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$16$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139547/selection/09827478f86a631e3e2a9e6df0b0566456ffb4ebf14639ba4d78bc873fbe0099" :answer/id #uuid "5d1d7ee4-9447-5f7d-a4d3-7def6e537ae0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$5$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139547/selection/d9ea69dc9cb6f4407349a5d53b4736f875008894a4883088ddba516383a2d25a" :answer/id #uuid "42d8d06f-45f5-5a34-a3ec-16696eb1b0bd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$8$ and $5$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139547/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "bcba808c-3c33-5777-8c48-ea8fd82b3cfd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-139547/selection/d9ea69dc9cb6f4407349a5d53b4736f875008894a4883088ddba516383a2d25a" :answer-field/choices ["q-139547/selection/67cb1be3333a3e5358177cb47769fab9690031e0294337deafaceade6cce27d9" "q-139547/selection/41b96c35deefeefa69699fe65f701eef7958190ba060ad602f8d813ac5c24c92" "q-139547/selection/78d9ad3ee5adf79dd4a92b184547ad36db0708eb90cb46f51b56f5ad263f9dec" "q-139547/selection/09827478f86a631e3e2a9e6df0b0566456ffb4ebf14639ba4d78bc873fbe0099" "q-139547/selection/d9ea69dc9cb6f4407349a5d53b4736f875008894a4883088ddba516383a2d25a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-139547" :question/id #uuid "e0a080ae-086d-5da1-af59-3299d460a5c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-139547" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/assets/d4947e37239d2ac2950b536d7afc2d0810ea713c437b7095a43e123d50ff2b34.png)\n\nA $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ what are the nonzero singular values of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=8={\\sigma }_{1},$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=5={\\sigma }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=8$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-139547/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cec3c21-28df-52ba-9b78-86cffb4c7720"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-139547"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-139548/selection/cf9db104a57b993779b8bb712bccd0fb95d16633f5962222a48e297e635f13a5" :answer/id #uuid "d71cfe5b-ea39-51d2-9e03-357cb2829cee" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\sqrt{7}$ and $10$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139548/selection/17ef050ba96d0f3d8605f2ba216502d6b2dba3f99bdd9fdb8086586c1e7c82e4" :answer/id #uuid "a4a0a15d-5c4e-5c72-84b4-dd289af78a85" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$7{v}_{1}$ and $5{v}_{1}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139548/selection/7a908154731ee8e977cb3afe2159947e3ca1b623a5d660c73b669f3232370aeb" :answer/id #uuid "0c802013-3109-5794-86d7-d89ff11a04fc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$7$ and $5$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139548/selection/67cb1be3333a3e5358177cb47769fab9690031e0294337deafaceade6cce27d9" :answer/id #uuid "93363083-3d10-5eec-858d-a7b261cb04a9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{1}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139548/selection/545923fab2ebfc5b33933e8d67f59d7dfab8b80a24cc1d88b5eb566026b11a72" :answer/id #uuid "6f7ed6c9-f312-502d-8873-97cbfe81ea1b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$7$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139548/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "16d49b84-9aeb-5bd3-a5a3-d25a59ab40b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-139548/selection/7a908154731ee8e977cb3afe2159947e3ca1b623a5d660c73b669f3232370aeb" :answer-field/choices ["q-139548/selection/cf9db104a57b993779b8bb712bccd0fb95d16633f5962222a48e297e635f13a5" "q-139548/selection/17ef050ba96d0f3d8605f2ba216502d6b2dba3f99bdd9fdb8086586c1e7c82e4" "q-139548/selection/7a908154731ee8e977cb3afe2159947e3ca1b623a5d660c73b669f3232370aeb" "q-139548/selection/67cb1be3333a3e5358177cb47769fab9690031e0294337deafaceade6cce27d9" "q-139548/selection/545923fab2ebfc5b33933e8d67f59d7dfab8b80a24cc1d88b5eb566026b11a72"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-139548" :question/id #uuid "09e8fde5-4515-5443-a9d5-56beb97a96e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-139548" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/assets/6be1f51600ae7b42ad0c4c8f67eb8f3ba3570606ec4e3e5e75c65df397aab060.png)\n\nA $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ what are the nonzero singular values of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=7={\\sigma }_{1},$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=5={\\sigma }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=7$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-139548/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cec3c21-28df-52ba-9b78-86cffb4c7720"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-139548"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9907"] :question/problem "A $2 \\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Given that the eigenvalues of $A^T A$ are $7, 2,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the ellipse $\\big\\{ A\\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $A\\mathbf{x}$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}^T (A^T A) \\mathbf{x}$ is the quadratic form with the matrix $A^T A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix $A^T A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of $A^T A$ is $7,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\|^2 \\big\\} = 7\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\| \\big\\}  = \\sqrt{7}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{7}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9909"] :question/problem "A $2 \\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are $\\sigma_1=4,$ $\\sigma_2=2,$ and $\\sigma_3 = 0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the ellipse $\\big\\{ A\\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $A\\mathbf{x}$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}^T (A^T A) \\mathbf{x}$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix $A^T A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix $A^T A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of $A^T A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is $\\sigma_1=4,$ the largest eigenvalue of $A^T A$ must be $\\lambda_1 = \\sigma_1^2=4^2.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\|^2 \\big\\} = 4^2\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\| \\big\\} = 4.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sigma_1=4.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9908"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/assets/094b6043b9cce7f3ac2a7cc32fa53785d3fbf5b490ce853474f89bb4afa8291a.png)\n\nA $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ are unit eigenvectors of $A^TA,$ find an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $36$ of $A^T A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\mathbf v_1, \\mathbf v_2\\,\\ldots, \\mathbf v_n\\right\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of $\\mathbb R^n$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $A^T A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1\\geq \\lambda_2\\geq \\ldots \\geq \\lambda_n.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- $\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1}$ is the length of $A\\mathbf v_1,$\n\n- $\\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2}$ is the length of $A\\mathbf v_2,$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A\\mathbf v_1|| = 8 = \\sigma_1,$ which means that $\\lambda_1 = 8^2 = 64,$ and\n\n- $||A\\mathbf v_2|| = 6 = \\sigma_2,$ which means that $\\lambda_2 = 6^2 = 36.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_2 = 36$ of $A^T A$ is $\\mathbf{v}_2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
